A||||1||Austin||Will removing the cat hurt my car?||||5||
Z||000000||Austin||12-22-2001||02:39 PM||||Will straight piping my cat out of the exhaust system hurt my car in anyway?  Does it make the exhaust sound louder?  I've heard of people saying it improves performance and I need a new cat, so I thought I could just bypass.  Here is something I've seen on eBay.  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=599453316" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=599453316</A> <P>Does anyone have this?  Does it work well?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Patrick's Dad||12-22-2001||03:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Austin:<BR><B>Will straight piping my cat out of the exhaust system hurt my car in anyway?</B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, this will not hurt your car in any way.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B>Does it make the exhaust sound louder?[/B[ [/QUOTE}<P>No cat? Not by itself, because the cat does little, if any, noise suppression. That's the muffler's job. No muffler? Yeah, it can get loud, but, depending on other things, not too oppressive. See below.<P>[QUOTE] [B]I've heard of people saying it improves performance and I need a new cat, so I thought I could just bypass.</B> <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>With less backpressure (exhaust gas filling up a chamber, then having to be stuffed back into a pipe) there is a modest performance gain, all other things being equal. See more below.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <B>Here is something I've seen on eBay.  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=599453316" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=599453316</A> <P>Does anyone have this?  Does it work well?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I looked at the eBay thing, and it's no special cat. The Fiero does not take a "special" cat, either, but you can change things around a little.<P>Consider your state's emissions laws. MA's toughened, somewhat on the California idea, so it's not something I want to take too many chances on. MA's noise laws will also become more socialist, er, Californian. In fact, The People's Replublic of Cambridge MA passed a noise ordinance well before anyone else. My setup was OK, but I did as little as I could to draw attention with the Police nearby.<P>My setup: IRM enlarged cat with 2½" outlet, no muffler, four Hooker resonator tips.<P>The cat, along with other standard enhancement techniques, allowed my 1988 to pass emissions for 1995 cars. Because the outlet pipe is larger than stock (by only ¼"), it flowed more freely, as well, and, with no muffler, had no second set of obstructions. The resonator tips sounded terrific once the engine warmed up. They didn't do too much cold, but did they mellow out nicely, at least until they started rusting off....<P>When I build my 3.4, I'm going to stick with the enlarged cat and look into a muffler I saw in Summit's catalog. You can unplug it (straight) for competition, then plug it back up for street. OTOH, there is a Y pipe available that you can do this with, too, but, by taking five or six feet of pipe out, I imagine it will get VERY loud.<P>There are also forum members with true dual exhausts. I don't know if any of them are running pairs of cats.||||reg||1
